      "path": "tools/wording-scan.ts",
      "content": "This matches quote-delimited substrings anywhere in the source, not just whole string literals. For example, `\u003cp\u003eThe label 'not closed yet' means the trade is still open.\u003c/p\u003e` and `const help = \"The label 'not closed yet' means the trade is still open.\";` both produce a hit, although neither the whole JSX text node nor the whole string literal equals a reason. This violates the whole-value restriction and can reject legitimate explanatory sentences. Match against actual string-literal/JSX-text token boundaries (and add regression tests for embedded quotes) rather than treating every quote as a literal delimiter.",
      "existing_code": "const reasonStringRe = new RegExp(`(['\"\\`])\\\\s*(${reasonAlt})\\\\s*\\\\1`, 'gi');",
      "start_line": 61,
      "end_line": 61,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "tools/wording-scan.ts",
      "content": "This only detects JSX text bounded by tags, but a whole JSX text node can also be bounded by an expression container. For example, `\u003cp\u003e{icon}No open position\u003c/p\u003e` contains a text node whose trimmed value is exactly a reason, yet this regex misses it because the preceding character is `}`, not `\u003e`. Similarly, `\u003cp\u003eNot closed yet{icon}\u003c/p\u003e` is missed. Match actual JSX text-node boundaries (preferably using the TSX parser) so these cases also fail the wording check.",
      "existing_code": "const reasonJsxRe = new RegExp(`\u003e\\\\s*(${reasonAlt})\\\\s*\u003c`, 'gi');",
